---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Short-Duration vs Long-Duration Contracts" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Short-Duration Contracts — Premium Revenue and Loss Reserve Model" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Long-Duration Contracts (Traditional) — the Net Premium Reserve Method" },
        { id: "s5", label: "LDTI (ASU 2018-12) Targeted Improvements" },
        { id: "s6", label: "THE KEY DIVERGENCE — IFRS 17's Fundamentally Different Measurement Architecture" },
        { id: "s7", label: "IFRS 17's General Measurement Model — Fulfillment Cash Flows Plus CSM" },
        { id: "s8", label: "IFRS 17's Premium Allocation Approach (PAA)" },
        { id: "s9", label: "IFRS 17's Variable Fee Approach (VFA)" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Deferred Acquisition Costs (DAC) — Amortization Contrast" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Worked Example — Short-Duration Premium/Reserve Recognition" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Worked Example — LDTI's Discount Rate OCI Mechanic" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Worked Example — Conceptual Contrast With CSM Release" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s16", label: "ASC 944 vs IFRS 17 — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s18", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s19", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Two very different contract durations, two very different models</div><div>Insurance contracts range from SHORT-DURATION property/casualty policies (annual auto or homeowners coverage) to LONG-DURATION life, annuity, and long-term care contracts spanning DECADES. ASC 944 addresses these with genuinely DIFFERENT measurement models — a relatively simple unearned-premium approach for short-duration contracts, and a more complex reserve/DAC model (recently updated by LDTI) for long-duration contracts.</div></div>
    <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">One of the most significant global accounting divergences of the modern era</div><div>IFRS 17 — effective 2023, after nearly two decades of development — represents a WHOLESALE replacement of prior international insurance accounting practice with a fundamentally NEW measurement architecture, built around FULFILLMENT CASH FLOWS and a CONTRACTUAL SERVICE MARGIN (Sections 6-9). US GAAP, in contrast, RETAINED its traditional model with TARGETED improvements (LDTI) rather than a comparable ground-up rebuild — making this one of the most SUBSTANTIAL, well-publicised accounting divergences between the two frameworks in recent standard-setting history.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Short-Duration vs Long-Duration Contracts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A foundational classification driving which model applies</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Contract type</span><span>Description</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Short-duration</span><span>Provides coverage for a FIXED, typically SHORT period (e.g. annual property/casualty policies), with the insurer able to CANCEL or adjust terms at renewal</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Long-duration</span><span>Provides coverage over an EXTENDED, often multi-decade period (life insurance, annuities, long-term care), typically NOT cancellable by the insurer and with premiums fixed at issuance</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Short-Duration Contracts — Premium Revenue and Loss Reserve Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Premium earned over the coverage period, losses reserved on a best-estimate, undiscounted basis</div><div>PREMIUM revenue is recognised RATABLY over the CONTRACT (coverage) period, with any UNEARNED portion carried as an UNEARNED PREMIUM RESERVE liability. Claim LOSS reserves are established for INCURRED losses (both reported and "incurred but not reported," IBNR) at MANAGEMENT'S BEST ESTIMATE of the ultimate settlement amount — GENERALLY on an UNDISCOUNTED basis (for most lines), reflecting the traditionally conservative approach to short-duration reserve estimation.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Long-Duration Contracts (Traditional) — the Net Premium Reserve Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Locked-in assumptions at issuance, historically</div><div>Under the TRADITIONAL long-duration model, a NET PREMIUM RESERVE is established using assumptions (mortality, morbidity, interest, persistency) LOCKED IN at CONTRACT ISSUANCE — historically NOT routinely updated for actual experience unless a loss recognition ("premium deficiency") event occurred. Deferred acquisition costs (DAC) were historically amortised in PROPORTION to estimated gross profits/margins, requiring ongoing profit projections. LDTI (Section 5) substantially reformed both of these mechanics.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>LDTI (ASU 2018-12) Targeted Improvements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Targeted, not wholesale, reform — updated assumptions, current discount rates through OCI, simplified DAC</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>LDTI improvement</span><span>What changed</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Assumption updates</span><span>Cash flow assumptions (mortality, morbidity, persistency) for the net premium ratio must now be reviewed and updated AT LEAST ANNUALLY, with the effect recognised in NET INCOME — replacing the old locked-in-at-issuance approach</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Discount rate</span><span>A CURRENT, upper-medium-grade FIXED-INCOME discount rate is now used to discount the liability, with the EFFECT of discount rate CHANGES recognised in <strong>OCI</strong> (not net income) — separating economic discount rate volatility from the net income effect of underlying assumption changes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Market risk benefits</span><span>Certain features providing protection against capital market risk (e.g. living/death benefit guarantees on variable annuities) are now measured at <strong>FAIR VALUE</strong>, with changes attributable to the entity's OWN credit risk in OCI</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>DAC amortization</span><span>SIMPLIFIED to a level, generally STRAIGHT-LINE basis over the expected contract term — no longer tied to projected gross profits/margins, removing a significant source of estimation complexity and earnings volatility</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">LDTI is a genuinely SUBSTANTIAL reform of the traditional model — but it remains a TARGETED set of improvements layered onto the EXISTING net premium reserve architecture, rather than a wholesale replacement of the kind IFRS 17 represents.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — IFRS 17's Fundamentally Different Measurement Architecture</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A completely different starting architecture, not just updated assumptions</div><div>IFRS 17 REPLACED the prior patchwork international standard (IFRS 4) with an ENTIRELY NEW measurement architecture applicable to ALL insurance contracts (not separately bifurcated by short/long duration as the primary classification driver): a CURRENT, PROBABILITY-WEIGHTED estimate of FUTURE CASH FLOWS, DISCOUNTED using CURRENT rates, PLUS an explicit RISK ADJUSTMENT for non-financial risk, PLUS a CONTRACTUAL SERVICE MARGIN (CSM) representing UNEARNED PROFIT that is released to income GRADUALLY as insurance coverage is PROVIDED over the contract's life (Section 7). This is a GENUINELY different STRUCTURAL approach from ANYTHING in ASC 944 — even POST-LDTI, US GAAP's model remains recognisably built on the traditional NET PREMIUM/DAC architecture, whereas IFRS 17 discards that architecture ENTIRELY in favour of the fulfillment-cash-flow-plus-CSM framework. This is widely regarded as one of the most SIGNIFICANT, closely-watched global accounting divergences of the past decade, given IFRS 17's unusually long (nearly 20-year) development process and its comprehensive scope.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>IFRS 17's General Measurement Model — Fulfillment Cash Flows Plus CSM</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Profit recognised as coverage is provided, not at contract inception</div><div>Under IFRS 17's General Measurement Model (GMM, also called the "Building Block Approach"), the insurance contract liability equals FULFILLMENT CASH FLOWS (probability-weighted, discounted expected future cash flows, plus a risk adjustment) PLUS the CONTRACTUAL SERVICE MARGIN (CSM) — a SEPARATE component representing the UNEARNED PROFIT expected from the contract, explicitly PREVENTING day-one profit recognition. The CSM is systematically RELEASED to profit or loss OVER THE COVERAGE PERIOD, as insurance services are actually PROVIDED — a fundamentally different profit emergence pattern than ASC 944's traditional reserve/DAC model produces.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>IFRS 17's Premium Allocation Approach (PAA)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A simplification available for shorter-duration-like contracts</div><div>For contracts with a COVERAGE PERIOD of ONE YEAR OR LESS (or where the PAA would produce a result NOT materially different from the full GMM), IFRS 17 permits the PREMIUM ALLOCATION APPROACH — a SIMPLIFIED model resembling, in broad spirit, ASC 944's short-duration unearned premium approach. This is the area of GREATEST practical convergence between the two frameworks — though even here, IFRS 17's PAA still applies IFRS 17's OWN discounting and risk adjustment mechanics for the liability for incurred claims, features not always present in ASC 944's traditional undiscounted short-duration reserve approach.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>IFRS 17's Variable Fee Approach (VFA)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A specialised variant for participating/investment-linked contracts</div><div>For contracts with DIRECT participation features (broadly, where policyholders share in the returns of specified underlying investment items), IFRS 17 provides the VARIABLE FEE APPROACH — a MODIFICATION of the general model treating the entity's share of underlying item returns as a variable FEE for service, with CSM adjustments reflecting changes in that fee — a specialised mechanism WITHOUT a close US GAAP parallel, given the different treatment of participating/variable contracts under ASC 944.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Deferred Acquisition Costs (DAC) — Amortization Contrast</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A separately tracked asset under US GAAP vs embedded in the CSM under IFRS 17</div><div>Under ASC 944 (post-LDTI), DAC continues to be recognised as a SEPARATE asset, amortised on a simplified straight-line basis. Under IFRS 17, acquisition costs are NOT recognised as a separate asset at all — instead, they are INCLUDED directly within the fulfillment cash flows used to determine the CSM, effectively REDUCING the CSM (and therefore future profit recognition) rather than being tracked and amortised as their OWN distinct asset — a genuinely different presentational and mechanical treatment of the same underlying economic cost.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Short-Duration Premium/Reserve Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Ratable earning over the coverage period</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A one-year auto insurance policy</div><div class="wk-body">An insurer writes a one-year auto policy on July 1, collecting a $1,200 annual premium upfront. By calendar year-end (6 months into the policy), the insurer has incurred $500 of claims on this policy.</div><div class="wk-answer">PREMIUM revenue recognised for the 6 months = $1,200 × (6/12) = <strong>$600 earned</strong>, with the remaining <strong>$600 carried as an unearned premium reserve</strong>. Separately, the insurer recognises <strong>$500 of incurred claims</strong> as an expense, with a corresponding loss reserve liability for any unpaid portion.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — LDTI's Discount Rate OCI Mechanic</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Separating discount rate effects from net income</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A long-duration liability remeasured for a discount rate change</div><div class="wk-body">A life insurer's long-duration reserve is $50,000,000, discounted at a current upper-medium-grade fixed-income rate. Market rates decline during the period, causing the SAME cash flows to discount to $52,000,000 (a $2,000,000 increase attributable PURELY to the lower discount rate, with no change in the underlying cash flow assumptions themselves).</div><div class="wk-answer">The $2,000,000 increase attributable to the DISCOUNT RATE change is recognised in <strong>OCI</strong>, NOT net income — under LDTI's explicit separation of discount-rate-driven remeasurement from net-income-affecting cash flow assumption changes, giving readers a clearer view of the insurer's underlying UNDERWRITING performance apart from interest rate volatility.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example — Conceptual Contrast With CSM Release</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where the SAME contract's profit would emerge differently</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A 20-year life insurance contract's expected total profit</div><div class="wk-body">A 20-year life insurance contract is expected to generate $4,000,000 of total lifetime profit.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Under ASC 944 (post-LDTI):</strong> profit emerges through the interplay of premium income, benefit reserve changes (using annually updated net premium ratios), and straight-line DAC amortisation — a pattern shaped by the traditional reserve architecture, WITHOUT a single explicit "unearned profit" balance being separately tracked and released.<br><strong>Under IFRS 17:</strong> the $4,000,000 of expected profit is captured EXPLICITLY as the CSM at inception, and released to profit or loss SYSTEMATICALLY over the 20-year coverage period as insurance services are provided — a transparent, explicitly profit-emergence-timed mechanism, structurally distinct from the US GAAP approach even where the total lifetime profit recognised is similar.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 944 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Liability for future policy benefits and market risk benefits, including a rollforward and disaggregated assumption update effects</span><span>Claims development information (short-duration contracts)</span><span>DAC balances and amortisation</span><span>Significant assumptions used to measure liabilities and their sensitivity</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classification of contracts as short- or long-duration</span><span>Identification of market risk benefits requiring fair value measurement</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Mortality, morbidity, and persistency assumptions, updated at least annually under LDTI</span><span>Claim loss reserves for short-duration contracts, including IBNR</span><span>Current discount rates applied to long-duration liabilities</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>ASC 944 vs IFRS 17 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 944 (post-LDTI) vs IFRS 17</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 944)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 17)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Overall architecture</span><span>Traditional net premium reserve model, targeted LDTI improvements</span><span>Wholesale new architecture — fulfillment cash flows plus CSM</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Day-one profit</span><span>Can emerge through DAC/reserve mechanics</span><span>EXPLICITLY prevented — deferred in the CSM, released over coverage</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Discount rate for long-duration liabilities</span><span>Current rate, changes through OCI (LDTI)</span><span>Current rate, with an accounting policy choice for where discount rate changes are recognised (P&amp;L or OCI)</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Acquisition costs</span><span>Separate DAC asset, straight-line amortisation</span><span>Included within fulfillment cash flows, reducing the CSM directly</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Short-duration-like simplification</span><span>Native short-duration model</span><span>Premium Allocation Approach (PAA) — the closest point of practical convergence</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Even after LDTI's substantial reforms, the two frameworks remain STRUCTURALLY different at their core — this is not a case of differing mechanics applied to a shared architecture, but two genuinely DIFFERENT architectures addressing the same underlying insurance economics.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 944 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Short-duration premium earning</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Ratable over the coverage period</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Long-duration cash flow assumption updates</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised in net income, at least annually</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Long-duration discount rate changes</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Recognised in OCI under LDTI</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>DAC amortisation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Straight-line over the expected contract term</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 220</span><span>Governs the OCI presentation framework LDTI's discount rate remeasurement flows through</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 820</span><span>Supplies the fair value measurement framework for market risk benefits</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Contractual service margin (CSM)</div><div class="glossary-def">An IFRS 17 component representing unearned profit on an insurance contract, released to profit or loss over the coverage period, with no direct US GAAP equivalent.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">LDTI (Long Duration Targeted Improvements)</div><div class="glossary-def">ASU 2018-12's targeted reforms to long-duration contract accounting, including annual assumption updates and OCI treatment of discount rate changes.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Market risk benefit</div><div class="glossary-def">A contract feature providing protection against capital market risk, measured at fair value under LDTI.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc944 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
